function maxKSubarraysWithBounds(A, k, L, R){
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) P[i] = P[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const neg = -1/0; const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(neg));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  const take = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      for(let len=L; len<=R && len<=i; len++){
        if (dp[i-len][t-1] !== neg) {
          const val = dp[i-len][t-1] + (P[i]-P[i-len]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = len; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {maxSum: dp[n][k], take}
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem tests dynamic programming with turn constraints on grid paths, requiring careful state design and path reconstruction. It evaluates understanding of edge case handling and performance optimization for large inputs.

## Key Concepts

- **4D DP state**: position (i, j), turn count (t), and ending direction (d)
- **Two transition types**: continuing in the same direction versus making a turn
- **Path reconstruction**: using parent pointers or backtracking to retrieve the actual path
- **Time and space trade-offs**: consider optimization for large grids

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(M, k) {
  const n = M.length;
  const m = M[0].length;
  const dp = Array(n).fill(null).map(() =>
    Array(m).fill(null).map(() =>
      Array(k + 1).fill(null).map(() => [-1, -1])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = M[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
          if (dp[i][j][t][d] < 0) continue;

          if (i + 1 < n) {
            const newD = 1;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i + 1][j]
              );
            }
          }

          if (j + 1 < m) {
            const newD = 0;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i][j + 1]
              );
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.max(dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][0], dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][1]);
}
```

## Complexity

- **Time**: O(n × m × k)
- **Space**: O(n × m × k)

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP over partitioning with an order-dependent gain, plus reconstruction. Distinct from standard sum-of-maximums or cooldown problems.

## Key Concepts

- Order-dependent block weighting
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- 2D DP with backtracking pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and reconstruction

## Code Example

